card.statement:
A functor $\Phi:\bD_1\rightarrow\bD_2$ between triangulated categories is called spherical if it admits the left adjoint $\Phi^*$ and the right adjoint $\Phi^{!}$ such that the following natural transformations are isomorphisms of functors:

\begin{equation*}
\begin{array}{c}
\begin{tikzcd}
\Phi^{!}\oplus \Phi^{*} \arrow[rr,"\eta\circ\Phi^!+\Phi^*\circ\eta"] & & \Phi^{*}\circ\Phi\circ\Phi^{!},
\end{tikzcd} \\
\begin{tikzcd}
\Phi^{!}\oplus\Phi^{*} & &
\arrow[ll,"\Phi^!\circ \varepsilon + \varepsilon\circ \Phi"']
\Phi^{!}\circ\Phi\circ\Phi^{*},
\end{tikzcd}
\end{array}
\end{equation*}

where $\eta$ and $\varepsilon$ denote the unit and the counit morphisms associated with adjunctions.
